1
00:00:09,732 --> 00:00:15,132
You sit in a boat, and the shore slides
past. Is the shore moving? Or the boat?

2
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:16,943
Or is that the wrong question?

3
00:00:17,752 --> 00:00:20,380
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:24,338
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:25,148 --> 00:00:28,551
Eihei Dōgen lived from 1200 to 1253.

6
00:00:28,631 --> 00:00:31,553
As a young monk he went
from Japan to Song China,

7
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:34,067
practised there under
the Chan master Rujing,

8
00:00:34,147 --> 00:00:38,102
and came home to found what became
the Sōtō school of Japanese Zen.

9
00:00:38,182 --> 00:00:42,450
His great work, the Shōbōgenzō, the
Treasury of the True Dharma Eye,

10
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:46,476
is a collection of talks and essays
written over some twenty years.

11
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,855
In Part 6, I asked how the
seen can be just the seen,

12
00:00:50,935 --> 00:00:53,386
complete, and yet wholly dependent.

13
00:00:53,466 --> 00:00:55,778
Dōgen's answer is total exertion.

14
00:00:55,858 --> 00:00:58,907
And he gives it in images,
more than in arguments.

15
00:00:59,716 --> 00:01:03,037
I take four of his images,
and each has its own taste.

16
00:01:03,117 --> 00:01:04,912
Here, the first two.

17
00:01:04,992 --> 00:01:10,197
The shore that seems to move, and the boat
that carries its rower. Both are boats.

18
00:01:10,277 --> 00:01:14,355
And both are about who, if
anyone, stands outside the event.

19
00:01:14,435 --> 00:01:16,882
The shore takes away a seer outside.

20
00:01:16,962 --> 00:01:19,468
The boat takes away a doer behind.

21
00:01:20,264 --> 00:01:21,968
First, the shore.

22
00:01:22,836 --> 00:01:26,655
In the Genjōkōan, the opening
essay of the Shōbōgenzō,

23
00:01:26,735 --> 00:01:29,309
Dōgen describes someone riding in a boat.

24
00:01:29,389 --> 00:01:32,448
Looking at the shore, they
think the shore is moving.

25
00:01:32,528 --> 00:01:36,596
Looking closely at the boat, they
see that it is the boat that moves.

26
00:01:37,408 --> 00:01:42,002
In the same way, he says, when we take up
the myriad things with a confused body and

27
00:01:42,082 --> 00:01:46,150
mind, we suppose our own mind
and nature are permanent.

28
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:49,438
Practise closely, and
return to where you are,

29
00:01:49,518 --> 00:01:52,024
and it is clear that
nothing has a fixed self.

30
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:56,046
How is this usually read?
The boat is the self.

31
00:01:56,126 --> 00:01:58,641
Because we take ourselves
as the still centre,

32
00:01:58,721 --> 00:02:01,032
the world seems to change around us.

33
00:02:01,112 --> 00:02:04,909
Modern Sōtō teachers,
such as Shohaku Okumura,

34
00:02:05,012 --> 00:02:09,266
read it this way. We see everything
from our own self centred point of view.

35
00:02:09,346 --> 00:02:12,531
And practice returns us
to where we actually are.

36
00:02:13,344 --> 00:02:14,722
Here is how I see it.

37
00:02:15,916 --> 00:02:19,382
When I sit in a boat and the
shore seems to slide past,

38
00:02:19,462 --> 00:02:21,384
nothing mysterious has happened.

39
00:02:21,464 --> 00:02:24,509
And nothing mysterious is
needed to set it right.

40
00:02:24,589 --> 00:02:27,584
Motion is always motion
relative to something.

41
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:32,020
The habit gives motion an
owner. First the shore moves.

42
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,506
Then, corrected, the boat moves.

43
00:02:34,586 --> 00:02:37,278
But that second step is
still the same habit.

44
00:02:37,358 --> 00:02:39,765
The motion has only changed owners.

45
00:02:39,845 --> 00:02:43,410
And now the shore has become
the fixed ground, instead of me.

46
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:46,468
Anatta takes away the still watcher.

47
00:02:46,548 --> 00:02:49,488
There is just the seeing,
with no one behind it.

48
00:02:50,296 --> 00:02:51,851
But that is only half.

49
00:02:51,931 --> 00:02:56,525
The seer is not lost in the seen, as
if a scene were left with no one in it.

50
00:02:56,605 --> 00:02:58,223
The seer is in the seen.

51
00:02:58,303 --> 00:03:00,112
And the seen is in the seer.

52
00:03:00,192 --> 00:03:02,367
Neither is found outside the other.

53
00:03:02,447 --> 00:03:04,867
Nāgārjuna found the same with seeing.

54
00:03:04,947 --> 00:03:07,394
No seer is found without the seeing.

55
00:03:07,474 --> 00:03:09,573
And no seeing without a seer.

56
00:03:10,388 --> 00:03:13,193
And dependent arising
shows what the moving is.

57
00:03:13,273 --> 00:03:16,128
It is singled out between boat and shore,

58
00:03:16,208 --> 00:03:21,997
water and eyes, all at once. No one
owns it. Not the shore. Not the boat.

59
00:03:22,077 --> 00:03:25,706
Not the seer. There is no
mover apart from the moving.

60
00:03:25,786 --> 00:03:27,925
And no moving apart from a mover.

61
00:03:28,728 --> 00:03:32,103
That is the taste of this
image. No seat outside.

62
00:03:32,183 --> 00:03:36,727
One moving, with nothing outside it,
and nowhere left over to watch from.

63
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:41,785
I do not have to wait for some later
insight to taste total exertion here.

64
00:03:41,865 --> 00:03:46,364
The substantialist view, anatta, and
dependent arising are all in this one

65
00:03:46,444 --> 00:03:47,999
image, at once.

66
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,069
And where I part.

67
00:03:50,149 --> 00:03:54,526
The usual reading stops at the self
that sets itself up as the still centre.

68
00:03:54,606 --> 00:03:56,319
I go one step further.

69
00:03:56,399 --> 00:03:59,078
It is really the boat, is still the habit,

70
00:03:59,158 --> 00:04:01,949
if the motion is simply
handed to a new owner.

71
00:04:02,748 --> 00:04:04,846
Second, rowing the boat.

72
00:04:05,656 --> 00:04:08,814
The second boat is in
another essay, Zenki.

73
00:04:08,894 --> 00:04:10,680
Total Dynamic Working.

74
00:04:11,492 --> 00:04:14,949
Life, Dōgen says, is
like riding in a boat.

75
00:04:15,029 --> 00:04:17,829
You raise the sail, and
steer with the pole.

76
00:04:17,909 --> 00:04:21,851
In Kazuaki Tanahashi's
translation: Although you row,

77
00:04:21,931 --> 00:04:25,819
the boat gives you a ride, and
without the boat no one could ride.

78
00:04:25,899 --> 00:04:29,750
But you ride in the boat, and your
riding makes the boat what it is.

79
00:04:30,552 --> 00:04:34,593
At such a moment, he goes on, there
is only the world of the boat.

80
00:04:34,673 --> 00:04:38,248
Sky, water and shore all
belong to the boat's moment.

81
00:04:38,328 --> 00:04:41,971
And that moment is not the same
as a moment that is not the boat.

82
00:04:42,051 --> 00:04:44,688
So life is the total working of life.

83
00:04:44,768 --> 00:04:47,519
And death is the total working of death.

84
00:04:48,316 --> 00:04:50,020
How is this usually read?

85
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,208
Self and world are not two.

86
00:04:52,288 --> 00:04:54,844
I make the boat, and the boat makes me.

87
00:04:54,924 --> 00:04:58,594
Some modern teachers widen
this into one universal life.

88
00:04:58,674 --> 00:05:04,191
Kōshō Uchiyama, for example, speaks of a
self that includes everything it meets.

89
00:05:04,271 --> 00:05:07,181
So that my life is the
whole universe, living.

90
00:05:07,261 --> 00:05:10,129
Other readers stay
closer to the single act.

91
00:05:10,209 --> 00:05:12,498
This rowing, fully carried out.

92
00:05:13,304 --> 00:05:14,682
Here is how I see it.

93
00:05:15,876 --> 00:05:18,545
The habit sees a rower who drives a boat.

94
00:05:18,625 --> 00:05:21,154
A doer, and a thing being done to.

95
00:05:21,234 --> 00:05:25,669
Anatta takes the rower out from behind
the rowing. There is just rowing.

96
00:05:26,464 --> 00:05:28,358
But Dōgen says more than that.

97
00:05:28,438 --> 00:05:31,918
The rowing needs the boat as much
as the boat needs the rowing.

98
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:34,513
The rower is a rower only in the rowing.

99
00:05:34,593 --> 00:05:37,385
And the boat is a boat
only in being ridden.

100
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:43,566
Arm, oar, water, wind and wood do not
come together after they already exist.

101
00:05:43,646 --> 00:05:46,284
They are designated
together, in the one act.

102
00:05:46,364 --> 00:05:51,216
Agent and action, as Nāgārjuna says, are
established only in dependence on each

103
00:05:51,296 --> 00:05:52,496
other.

104
00:05:52,824 --> 00:05:57,327
The sky, the water and the shore belong
to this moment as its conditions.

105
00:05:57,407 --> 00:05:59,542
Fully, with nothing held back.

106
00:05:59,622 --> 00:06:03,441
They are not a bigger world, in
which the rowing is a small event.

107
00:06:03,521 --> 00:06:05,765
And Dōgen guards this himself.

108
00:06:05,845 --> 00:06:09,583
The boat's moment is not the same
as a moment that is not the boat.

109
00:06:09,663 --> 00:06:11,272
It stays this rowing.

110
00:06:12,076 --> 00:06:15,044
That is the taste of this image. No doer.

111
00:06:15,124 --> 00:06:17,802
Rowing and being carried, at once.

112
00:06:17,882 --> 00:06:20,859
There is effort, and no
one stands behind it.

113
00:06:20,939 --> 00:06:23,744
Nothing outside is
needed to finish the act.

114
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:28,954
And where I part. I do not follow
the reading of one universal life,

115
00:06:29,034 --> 00:06:31,699
or a universal self, living through me.

116
00:06:31,779 --> 00:06:34,022
That puts a Whole behind the rowing.

117
00:06:34,102 --> 00:06:36,337
And the rowing is complete without one.

118
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:39,320
Put the two boats side by side.

119
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:43,237
The shore takes away the
watcher outside the event.

120
00:06:43,317 --> 00:06:45,914
The rowing takes away the doer behind it.

121
00:06:45,994 --> 00:06:49,365
What is left is not an empty
event, with no one in it.

122
00:06:49,445 --> 00:06:54,166
It is the event, wholly itself,
with all its conditions taking part.

123
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:57,228
This changes what intimacy means.

124
00:06:57,308 --> 00:07:01,263
Ordinarily, intimacy means two
things that have come close.

125
00:07:01,343 --> 00:07:03,220
A hearer close to a sound.

126
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:05,190
A rower close to the work.

127
00:07:05,270 --> 00:07:07,993
Here, there were never
two to bring together.

128
00:07:08,808 --> 00:07:10,866
Hearing is not intimate with sound.

129
00:07:10,946 --> 00:07:15,716
Hearing is the sounding, with the ear,
the air and the bell all taking part.

130
00:07:15,796 --> 00:07:18,474
Breathing is not merely
connected to the air.

131
00:07:18,554 --> 00:07:22,999
Lungs, air, body, warmth and
movement are one breathing.

132
00:07:23,079 --> 00:07:26,450
Nothing has to bridge them,
because there was never a gap.

133
00:07:26,530 --> 00:07:29,362
I call this intimacy beyond relationship.

134
00:07:30,172 --> 00:07:34,757
And it is not fusion. The shore stays
the shore, and the boat the boat.

135
00:07:34,837 --> 00:07:36,632
The oar is not the water.

136
00:07:36,712 --> 00:07:38,547
The arm is not the wind.

137
00:07:38,627 --> 00:07:41,143
Each is distinct, and more vivid for it.

138
00:07:41,223 --> 00:07:45,291
What has gone is only the seat
outside, and the doer behind.

139
00:07:46,088 --> 00:07:47,684
So, which holds?

140
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:50,065
By experience.

141
00:07:50,145 --> 00:07:54,019
When I sit in a moving boat, is
motion met as something the shore has?

142
00:07:54,099 --> 00:07:57,117
Or the boat has? Or as
the relation itself?

143
00:07:57,197 --> 00:08:01,750
When I row, do I meet a rower standing
behind the rowing, giving it orders?

144
00:08:01,830 --> 00:08:03,856
What is met is the moving and the rowing,

145
00:08:03,936 --> 00:08:06,456
complete, with nothing outside them.

146
00:08:07,264 --> 00:08:11,156
By logic. If motion belonged
to the shore by its own nature,

147
00:08:11,236 --> 00:08:13,343
it could never be seen as the boat's.

148
00:08:13,423 --> 00:08:16,536
If the rower existed first,
apart from the rowing,

149
00:08:16,616 --> 00:08:18,846
it would not need the boat to be a rower.

150
00:08:18,926 --> 00:08:22,270
If the boat existed first,
apart from being ridden,

151
00:08:22,350 --> 00:08:24,580
it would not need the rower to be a boat.

152
00:08:24,660 --> 00:08:26,948
Each is what it is only with the other.

153
00:08:27,764 --> 00:08:30,718
By working. Nothing
works the less for this.

154
00:08:30,798 --> 00:08:32,648
The boat crosses the river.

155
00:08:32,728 --> 00:08:35,365
The rower still pulls, and arrives.

156
00:08:35,445 --> 00:08:37,856
The shore is still where the boat lands.

157
00:08:38,664 --> 00:08:42,375
So the view of a still watcher,
and a rower who drives a boat,

158
00:08:42,455 --> 00:08:47,805
is not the truer one. It is the default,
carried by habit, and by the word I.

159
00:08:48,604 --> 00:08:52,482
None of this makes my everyday
words false. I row the boat.

160
00:08:52,562 --> 00:08:54,194
I see the shore go by.

161
00:08:54,274 --> 00:08:59,484
What fails is only a seer, a rower,
or a boat, standing on its own.

162
00:09:00,296 --> 00:09:04,541
This is Section 2, Part 7 of
the Dependent Arising series.

163
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:08,294
In Part 8, I take Dōgen's image of time.

164
00:09:08,374 --> 00:09:11,392
Firewood and ash, each
in its own position,

165
00:09:11,472 --> 00:09:13,149
with nothing carried over.

